<p data-start="1689" data-end="1806" class="">Diese heiÃŸen <strong data-start="1702" data-end="1729">unendliche Wurzelreihen</strong>, weil die Exponenten <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/p^n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord">1/</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.6644em;"><span style="top: -3.063em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">n</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> sind â€“ also Wurzeln beliebig hoher Ordnung.</p>
<p data-start="1808" data-end="1842" class="">Diese Reihen leben in KÃ¶rpern wie:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="double-struck">F</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">/</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">âˆž</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{F}_p((t^{1/p^\infty}))</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.2241em; vertical-align: -0.2861em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathbb">F</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">p</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">â€‹</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2861em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mopen">((</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">t</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.938em;"><span style="top: -3.113em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mtight">1/</span><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight">p</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.7385em;"><span style="top: -2.931em; margin-right: 0.0714em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.5em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size3 size1 mtight"><span class="mord mtight">âˆž</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">))</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1880" data-end="2010" class="">= vollstÃ¤ndige Laurent-Reihen mit <strong data-start="1914" data-end="1944">infinitesimalen Exponenten</strong>.<br data-start="1945" data-end="1948">
Sie kodieren die Geometrie eines perfektoiden KÃ¶rpers im Tilt.</p>
<hr data-start="2012" data-end="2015" class="">
<h2 data-start="2017" data-end="2043" class=""><strong data-start="2020" data-end="2043">5. Diamond-Struktur</strong></h2>
<p data-start="2045" data-end="2211" class="">Ein <strong data-start="2049" data-end="2060">Diamond</strong> ist ein geometrischer Raum (in der Kategorie der v-perfectoid Spaces), der aus einem perfektoiden Objekt durch <strong data-start="2172" data-end="2201">pro-Ã©tale Quotientbildung</strong> entsteht.</p>
<p data-start="2213" data-end="2238" class=""><strong data-start="2213" data-end="2238">Motivation (Scholze):</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2239" data-end="2468">
<li data-start="2239" data-end="2329" class="">
<p data-start="2241" data-end="2329" class="">Klassische <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span></span></span></span>-adische Geometrie (z.â€¯B. formal schemes, adische RÃ¤ume) ist zu starr.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2330" data-end="2468" class="">
<p data-start="2332" data-end="2468" class="">Diamonds erlauben feinere Strukturen, besonders in positiver Charakteristik, etwa fÃ¼r <strong data-start="2418" data-end="2439">Ã©tale Kohomologie</strong> oder <strong data-start="2445" data-end="2467">Shimura-VarietÃ¤ten</strong>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2470" data-end="2649" class=""><strong data-start="2470" data-end="2481">Formal:</strong><br data-start="2481" data-end="2484">
Ein Diamond ist der <strong data-start="2504" data-end="2559">fppf-Sheaf auf der Kategorie der perfektoiden RÃ¤ume</strong>, der entsteht als Quotient eines perfekten Raums durch eine pro-Ã©tale Ã„quivalenzrelation.</p>
<p data-start="2651" data-end="2791" class="">Diamonds sind der â€žSpielplatzâ€œ der modernen arithmetischen Geometrie â€“ in ihnen vergleichen wir Tilts verschiedener <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span></span></span></span>-adischer KÃ¶rper.</p>
<hr data-start="2793" data-end="2796" class="">
<h2 data-start="2798" data-end="2822" class=""><strong data-start="2801" data-end="2822">6. Cartier-Moduln</strong></h2>
<p data-start="2824" data-end="2973" class=""><strong data-start="2824" data-end="2842">Cartier-Moduln</strong> sind algebraische Objekte, auf denen der <strong data-start="2884" data-end="2904">Cartier-Operator</strong> (aus der Theorie der Differentiale in Charakteristik <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span></span></span></span>) wirkt.</p>
<p data-start="2975" data-end="2990" class=""><strong data-start="2975" data-end="2990">Inhaltlich:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2991" data-end="3262">
<li data-start="2991" data-end="3100" class="">
<p data-start="2993" data-end="3100" class="">Sie beschreiben Strukturen von Differenzialformen und Kohomologien Ã¼ber KÃ¶rpern der Charakteristik <span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span></span></span></span>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3101" data-end="3262" class="">
<p data-start="3103" data-end="3136" class="">Besonders wichtig im Kontext von:</p>
<ul data-start="3139" data-end="3262">
<li data-start="3139" data-end="3171" class="">
<p data-start="3141" data-end="3171" class=""><strong data-start="3141" data-end="3171"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.625em; vertical-align: -0.1944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">p</span></span></span></span>-divisiblen Gruppen</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="3174" data-end="3204" class="">
<p data-start="3176" data-end="3204" class=""><strong data-start="3176" data-end="3204">cristalliner Kohomologie</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start="3207" data-end="3262" class="">
<p data-start="3209" data-end="3262" class=""><strong data-start="3209" data-end="3262">modularen Invarianten in positiver Charakteristik</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3264" data-end="3440" class=""><strong data-start="3264" data-end="3292">In perfektoider Theorie:</strong> Sie tauchen auf, wenn man die Tilt-Geometrie mit klassischen Strukturen (wie De-Rham-Kohomologie oder p-adischer Hodge-Theorie) in Beziehung setzt.</p>
<hr data-start="3442" data-end="3445" class="">
<h2 data-start="3447" data-end="3466" class=""><strong data-start="3450" data-end="3466">Zusammenhang</strong></h2>
<p data-start="3468" data-end="3497" class="">Stell dir folgendes Bild vor:</p>
